/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In a mechanical system the pilot's hand supplies the command and the rod or cable carries it.
   - A control law is the formula or table that turns pilot and sensor inputs into actuator commands.
   - FBW saves weight by deleting cables, pulleys, turnbuckles and rigging.
   - Early FBW was analog.
   - p is the failure probability of one lane in one flight hour; k is the number of independent lanes.
   - C(n, k) is the number of ways to choose k lanes out of n; the approximation holds when p is small.
   - Fly-by-light replaces the copper signal wires with optical fibre.
*/
