/* book exam traps: FBW removes the mechanical linkage, not hydraulic power; actuators still need hydraulic or electric power. | Artificial feel remains: sidestick springs and Q-feel create stick force, not airload feedback through cables. | Saying analog FBW had no computer. Analog FBW used analog computers; what digital FBW adds is sampling, software (which must be validated), built-in test and gain scheduling in software. | Fly-by-light is not wireless: optical fibre carries signals, while actuator power still comes by wire or hydraulic line. | Voting lanes improve availability; the usual design answer is not one perfect lane but independent lanes with comparison and isolation. */
