/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An automatic flight control system (AFCS) is a closed loop.
   - The sensors are gyros for attitude and rate, an inertial measurement unit (IMU) for acceleration and rate, air data for airspeed and altitude, and a radio altimeter for height above the ground during approach.
   - The inner loop is fast and holds attitude or rate: pitch rate, roll rate and yaw damping.
   - For the roll channel: δa is the aileron command in degrees; p is the roll rate in degrees per second, positive right.
   - Pitch hold and roll hold keep the current attitude.
   - Some autopilots add low-speed protection: when speed falls toward the stall they warn, change mode or disconnect.
   - Rate feedback exists because proportional control alone overshoots.
   - Many Cessna 172R aircraft carry a single-axis autopilot that works as a wing leveller: one servo on the ailerons and a turn coordinator as its rate sensor.
   - Active control technology goes further, using the same surfaces for load alleviation and stability augmentation; see the chapter on active control technology.
   - The auto-throttle system (ATS) moves the thrust levers.
*/
